    {\left\vert\kern-0.25ex\left\vert\kern-0.25ex\left\vert u - v 
    \right\vert\kern-0.25ex\right\vert\kern-0.25ex\right\vert}^2_{a,r_2} \leq 2\big(J(v) - J(u)\big),
\end{equation}
as in the linear case (see the proof of Theorem 3.2 in \cite{LiuCai2}). To this end, multiplying \cref{pde} by $w \in \{u, v\}$ and using integration by parts, we have
\begin{equation*}
f(w) = \int_0^1 \left(a\,u'w' + r_1\,u^3 w + r_2\,u w\right)dx + \alpha a(0)u'(0),
\end{equation*}
which implies
\begin{equation*}
f(v)-f(u) = \int_0^1 \left(a\,u'(v'-u') + r_1\,u^3 (v-u) + r_2\,u (v-u)\right)dx.
\end{equation*}

A straightforward calculation gives 
\begin{equation}\@bsphack
