Let $\hat{r}_i= 3\mu_1\min\limits_{x \in [b_{i-1}, b_{i+1}]}u_n^2(x) + \mu_2$.
For any vector $\mbox{\boldmath$\xi$} =(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_n)^T\in \mathbb{R}^{n}$, let 
\[
v(x)=\mbox{\boldmath$\xi$}^T {\bf D}_n(\hat{{\bf c}}){\bf H}_n(x) = \sum\limits_{j=1}^n c_j\xi_j H_j(x).
\]
It follows from the triangle inequality, the definitions of $\tilde{h}_i$ and $\hat{r}_i$, the fact that $v(x)$ is piecewise constant, and \cref{r} that 
\begin{align*}\notag
    \dfrac12 \sum_{i=1}^n \tilde{h}_i\hat{r}_i\Bigl(c_i\xi_i\Bigr)^2 & \leq \sum_{i=1}^n {h}_i\hat{r}_i\left(\sum_{j=1}^i c_j\xi_j \right)^2 + \sum_{i=2}^n {h}_{i-1}\hat{r}_i\left(\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} c_j\xi_j \right)^2 \\[2mm]
    &= \sum_{i=1}^n \hat{r}_i \int_{b_i}^{b_{i+1}}v^2(x)\,dx + \sum_{i=2}^n \hat{r}_i\int_{b_{i-1}}^{b_{i}}v^2(x)\,dx \leq 2 \int^1_0 r(x) v^2(x)\,dx,
\end{align*}
which, together with \cref{hessianB}, implies 
$$\mbox{\boldmath$\xi$}^T {\@fontswitch{\relax}{\mathcal} H}_{22}(\mbox{\boldmath${\theta}$})\mbox{\boldmath$\xi$} = \int^1_0 r(x) v^2(x)\,dx + \sum\limits_{i=1}^n c_ig_i \xi^2_i \geq  \sum\limits_{i=1}^n c_i^2\left[\frac{\tilde{h}_i \hat{r}_i}{4} + \dfrac{g_i}{c_i}\right] \xi_i^2.$$ 
Thus, the positive definiteness of ${\@fontswitch{\relax}{\mathcal} H}_{22}(\mbox{\boldmath${\theta}$})$ is a direct consequence of the assumptions. This completes the proof of the lemma.
